    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This essay won or received an honorable mention for The Boothe Prize for excellence in first-year writing. The Boothe Prize recognizes and rewards outstanding expository and argumentative writing by undergraduate students in first-year classes that satisfy the WR 1 requirement. In each award-winning essay, student writers demonstrate clarity of argument, excellent integration of research-based evidence, and compelling prose style.\r\nSierra Wells studies the songs and history of the San José-\r\nbased band, Los Tigres del Norte. With careful attention and remarkable range, she\r\n\r\ncharts how this legendary band, whose members immigrated to the United States in\r\n\r\nthe 1970s, offers critical insight into the individual and lived experience of border-\r\ncrossing over several decades.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Wells, S. (2024). De Paisano a Paisano: What a Legendary Mexican Immigrant Band Has to Say About the Lives of Its Listeners and Why It Matters. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/gm366fg9832. https://doi.org/10.25740/gm366fg9832.",
